          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which statement correctly describes the functional role of the nucleus ambiguus </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which statement correctly describes the functional role of the nucleus ambiguus in the medulla?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>It sends parasympathetic fibres to thoracic and abdominal viscera</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This describes the dorsal nucleus of the vagus, a parasympathetic (general visceral efferent) nucleus — not the nucleus ambiguus.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It receives taste and visceral afferent input from the tongue and pharynx</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This describes the nucleus tractus solitarius, which handles gustatory and visceral afferents. The nucleus ambiguus is a motor nucleus, not sensory.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It provides branchial motor innervation to pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The nucleus ambiguus is a special visceral efferent (branchial motor) nucleus that drives swallowing and phonation through CN IX, X, and the cranial root of XI.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It supplies all intrinsic and extrinsic muscles of the tongue</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Tongue muscles are supplied by the hypoglossal nucleus (CN XII). The nucleus ambiguus serves pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles, not the tongue.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The nucleus ambiguus is the branchial motor nucleus for CN IX, X, and XI cranial root, powering swallowing and phonation. Distinguish it from the dorsal vagal nucleus (parasympathetic) and the nucleus tractus solitarius (sensory)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. The nucleus ambiguus is clinically important — its damage causes dysphagia and hoarseness, hallmarks of lateral medullary syndrome."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
