          "label": "<p>In medial medullary (Dejerine) syndrome, which combination of structures is char</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In medial medullary (Dejerine) syndrome, which combination of structures is characteristically affected?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Spinal nucleus of CN V, vestibular nuclei, and inferior cerebellar peduncle</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>These are lateral medullary structures affected in Wallenberg syndrome (PICA territory). They lie outside the anterior spinal artery territory.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Nucleus ambiguus, spinothalamic tract, and descending sympathetic fibres</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>These structures also lie in the lateral medulla and are damaged in Wallenberg syndrome, not Dejerine syndrome.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Corticospinal pyramid, medial lemniscus, and hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. This triad produces contralateral hemiplegia (pyramid), contralateral loss of fine touch and proprioception (medial lemniscus), and ipsilateral tongue deviation (CN XII).</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Inferior olivary nucleus, dorsal vagal nucleus, and nucleus tractus solitarius</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The inferior olivary nucleus lies lateral to the pyramid, and the dorsal vagal nucleus and NTS are dorsal structures — none form part of the classic medial medullary triad.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The three structures in Dejerine syndrome are all in the medial medulla supplied by the anterior spinal artery: the pyramid (contralateral hemiplegia), medial lemniscus (contralateral proprioception and touch loss), and CN XII (ipsilateral tongue paralysis)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The medial medullary triad — pyramid, medial lemniscus, CN XII — follows the anterior spinal artery territory and produces a characteristic crossed motor pattern with ipsilateral tongue deviation."
                }
              ],
